card('islam/general/d-av-sonstiges-islam-2/c-paulus-islamischer-prophet',{
 title:"Is Paul the third messenger in 36:14?",
 k:"Qurʾān 36:14 · Tafsīr & Ibn Kathīr",
 blurb:"Christian apologists claim Paul is the third messenger of Sūrah Yā-Sīn; Ibn Kathīr and the Qurʾānic context show the messengers were sent by Allah to an ancient destroyed city, not by Jesus.",
 claim:"Qurʾān 36:14 proves Paul was a true messenger sent by Jesus and recognized by Islam.",
 tree:N(AVP,
  "Some tafsīrs mention Paul (Būlus) as the third messenger in Qurʾān 36:14 — doesn't that make Paul an Islamic prophet?",
  "No. The Qurʾān speaks of three unnamed messengers sent directly by Allah to an unnamed city that was wiped out by a single blast (36:29) — not Jesus's disciples. Ibn Kathīr refutes the Antioch-Paul identification as very weak, noting it derives solely from two weak Isrāʾīliyyāt chains with no prophetic hadith or Qurʾānic support.",
  "Qurʾān 36:14, Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim 2365c & Ibn Kathīr's refutation",
  ["The Qurʾānic passage (36:14): “We sent them two messengers, but they rejected both. So We reinforced [the two] with a third, and they declared, ‘We have indeed been sent to you [as messengers].’” Notice the pronoun “We” — Allah Himself is the Sender.",
   "Prophetic boundary (Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim 2365c): Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I am the closest of all people to Jesus, son of Mary, in this world and the next.” They asked: “How is that, Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “The prophets are paternal brothers; their mothers are different, but their religion is one. And there is no prophet/messenger between me and Jesus.” (Note: Some tafsīrs differed on whether the three in 36:14 were messengers of Jesus himself rather than direct prophets of Allah, which is the only opening apologists try to exploit alongside this hadith.)",
   "Ibn Kathīr's historical refutation (Antioch): Ibn Kathīr points out that in the time of Christ, Antioch believed in Christ and became one of the four great Christian patriarchates (Antioch, Jerusalem, Alexandria, Rome — later joined by Constantinople). By contrast, the city in Sūrah Yā-Sīn was completely annihilated after killing the believer: “It was only one single cry, and behold, they were extinguished” (Qurʾān 36:29). If the three messengers went to ancient Antioch and God destroyed it before it was later rebuilt and believed at Christ's time, there is no contradiction; but claiming the Qurʾānic story is the story of Christ's companions is weak both historically and because the Qurʾān's plain context requires them to be messengers of God."],
  ["Qurʾān 36:14, 29","Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim 2365c","Tafsīr Ibn Kathīr on Qurʾān 36:14"],
  [N(AVP,
    "What about the reports in classical tafsīr that explicitly name Shimʿōn, Yūḥannā, and Paul?",
    "Ibn Kathīr himself quotes those narrations only to reject them as “very weak,” tracing the authentic majority report to ancient Antioch under King Antiochus and messengers named Ṣādiq, Ṣadūq, and Shalūm.",
    "Why the “Paul” report is rejected",
    ["Majority early view (ancient Antioch): Ibn Kathīr notes it is widely reported from early and later generations that the town was Antioch — transmitted by Ibn Isḥāq from Ibn ʿAbbās, Kaʿb al-Aḥbār, and Wahb ibn Munabbih, and similarly from Burayda ibn al-Ḥuṣayb, ʿIkrima, Qatāda, al-Zuhrī, and others. Ibn Isḥāq reported from Ibn ʿAbbās, Kaʿb, and Wahb that an idol-worshipping king named Antiochus, son of Antiochus, ruled there; God sent him three messengers — Ṣādiq, Ṣadūq, and Shalūm — whom he rejected, proving they were messengers of Almighty God.",
     "Origin & weakness of the Paul variant: Qatāda claimed they were messengers of Christ, and Ibn Jarīr [al-Ṭabarī] narrated from Wahb ← Ibn Sulaymān ← Shuʿayb al-Jubāʾī that the first two were named Shimʿōn and Yūḥannā and the third Paul (Būlus) in Antioch. Ibn Kathīr immediately judges: “This view is very weak, because the inhabitants of Antioch, when Christ sent his three disciples to them, did not all reject them” (rather, Antioch believed).",
     "Only two weak Isrāʾīliyyāt chains: The Paul identification rests on merely two reports from weak transmitters drawn from Isrāʾīliyyāt (Jewish/Christian lore); tafsīr scholars merely recorded what circulated, and there is zero prophetic hadith or Qurʾānic verse identifying Paul.",
     "Final conclusion: The Qurʾān records an incident of an unnamed city to which Allah (glorified and exalted be He) sent three unnamed messengers — not Jesus (peace be upon him) — and the city was destroyed for rejecting them. That is all revelation tells us. There are no authentic Islamic reports recognizing Saul of Tarsus as a legitimate prophet; Christian apologists engage in desperate cherry-picking, whereas Paul's own letters expose him as a false prophet."],
    ["Tafsīr Ibn Kathīr 36:14","Tafsīr al-Ṭabarī (Shuʿayb al-Jubāʾī report)"],
    [],
    {facts:[["Majority Report","King Antiochus; Ṣādiq, Ṣadūq & Shalūm"],["Paul Report","2 weak Isrāʾīliyyāt chains only"],["Ibn Kathīr's Verdict","“Very weak” — Antioch believed in Christ"],["Hadith / Qurʾān","Zero prophetic backing for Paul"]],
     visual:{kind:'compare',
      left:{title:"Qurʾān 36:14–29",items:["Messengers sent directly by Allah (“We sent…”)","Residents rejected them and killed the believer","City totally annihilated by a single blast (36:29)"]},
      right:{title:"New Testament Antioch",items:["Disciples / Paul went to Roman-era Antioch","Antioch famously believed and became a major Patriarchate","Never annihilated for rejecting Christian messengers"]}}})],
  {facts:[["Verse","Qurʾān 36:14 & 36:29"],["Hadith","Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim 2365c (no prophet between Jesus & Muḥammad ﷺ)"],["Authority","Tafsīr Ibn Kathīr"],["Verdict","Story is not about Paul"]],
   quotes:[
    {by:"Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ",en:"The prophets are brothers in faith; they have different mothers, yet their religion is one. And there is no prophet between me and Jesus.",ref:"Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim 2365c"},
    {by:"Ibn Kathīr",en:"As for the claim that the story mentioned in the Qurʾān is the story of the companions of Christ, this view is weak because of what has been mentioned above. Moreover, the apparent context of the Qurʾān requires that these messengers were from God.",ref:"Tafsīr Ibn Kathīr on Qurʾān 36:14"}
   ]}),
 sum:"Qurʾān 36:14–29 describes three messengers sent directly by Allah to a city subsequently destroyed (36:29), whereas Ibn Kathīr dismisses the two weak Isrāʾīliyyāt reports naming Paul as historically and textually untenable."});

