/* 9. The Paraclete (John 14–16) (2,554 chars rebuilt in full!) */
const PRK=DR('deens-paraklete-exegesis-of-john-14','1368235621570838639');
card('christianity/islam-vs/d-dr-deens-paraklete-exegesis-of-john-14',{
 title:"The Paraclete (John 14–16)",
 k:"Prophecies · John 14–16",
 blurb:"Why “another Paraclete” who only comes after Jesus leaves, speaks what he hears, and abides via his teachings fits a human prophet — backed by Luke 16:29, John 15:7, Raymond Brown & NLT.",
 claim:"The Paraclete in John 14–16 must be the Holy Spirit, not Muḥammad ﷺ, because he “lives with you and will be in you” forever and the world cannot “see” him.",
 tree:N(PRK,
  "The Paraclete is the invisible Holy Spirit, not Muḥammad — John 14:17 says the world cannot see him, and he abides with the disciples forever.",
  "Every single feature of John 14–16 fits a coming human prophet, while Johannine idiom explains “abiding” and “seeing” spiritually: Jesus calls himself a paraclete (1 John 2:1) and promises “another” who cannot come until Jesus departs (16:7) — yet the Holy Spirit was already present in Luke 1:15 and 3:22. Moreover, Luke 16:29 and John 15:7 prove “having,” “hearing,” and “abiding” refer to a prophet's teachings and foreknowledge, not physical omnipresence.",
  "Why “another Paraclete” isn't the Holy Spirit",
  ["Conditional departure (John 16:7): “If I do not go away, the Paraclete will not come to you” (also John 15:26; 16:7: “I will send the Paraclete”). Yet the Holy Spirit was already active in Luke 1:15, 3:22, and throughout the Old Testament — so the Spirit's coming could not depend on Jesus's departure.",
   "Speaking only what he hears (John 16:13): “He will not speak on his own; he will speak whatever he hears” — matching the prophet-like-Moses criterion in Deuteronomy 18:18 and Qurʾān 53:3–4 (“Nor does he speak from [his own] inclination; it is only a revelation revealed”).",
   "“Another” Paraclete (John 14:16; 1 John 2:1): Since 1 John 2:1 explicitly calls Jesus himself a paraklētos, “another Paraclete” means another messenger/advocate of the same kind as Jesus — a human prophet.",
   "Patristic note: The channel also cites Hippolytus, Against Noetus, in its broader discussion of Spirit/Word distinctions."],
  ["John 14:16–17; 15:7, 26; 16:7–14","1 John 2:1","Luke 16:29; 1:15; 3:22","Deuteronomy 18:18","Raymond Brown, Gospel According to John","Hippolytus, Against Noetus"],
  [
   N(PRK,
    "Doesn't “he abides with you forever” (John 14:17) prove the Paraclete couldn't be a mortal prophet?",
    "In both Luke 16:29 and John 15:7, Jesus uses the exact same language of “possessing/hearing” prophets and words “abiding” inside believers to refer to enduring teachings, scripture, and foreknowledge — not literal bodily habitation.",
    "Luke 16:29 & John 15:7: how “abide” works in John",
    ["Luke 16:29 (“They have Moses and the Prophets”): In Jesus's parable, Abraham says “They have [literally: possess] Moses and the Prophets; let them hear [primary meaning: audibly] them.” First-century Jews obviously did not physically possess Moses or hear him audibly — they possessed his teachings, writings, and doctrines. Likewise, the Paraclete “being with” believers does not require literal physical presence, but refers to his teachings or foreknowledge of his coming.",
     "John 15:7 (Same Greek verb for “abide” as 14:17): In John 15:7, Jesus interchanges himself abiding in the disciples with “my words abide in you.” The Greek verb for “abide” (menō) in 15:7 is the exact same verb used in John 14:17. Because words cannot literally reside inside human bodies, every commentator reads this as teachings, revelation, or faith abiding forever — meaning John 14:17 refers to the Paraclete's enduring revelation/teachings or the disciples' foreknowledge of the Prophet's ﷺ mission."],
    ["Luke 16:29","John 14:17; 15:7"],
    [],
    {visual:{kind:'compare',
      left:{title:"Luke 16:29 & John 15:7",items:["“They have Moses & the Prophets, let them hear them”","“If my words abide (menō) in you…”","Universal reading: teachings, doctrine & foreknowledge"]},
      right:{title:"John 14:17 Applied Consistently",items:["“He abides (menō) with you and will be in you”","Same author, same verb (menō)","Refers to his enduring revelation & foreknowledge of his coming"]}}}),
   N(PRK,
    "What about John 14:17 saying the world “neither sees him nor knows him” — doesn't that mean an invisible spirit?",
    "No — even major Catholic scholar Raymond Brown and the NLT translation explain that “seeing” here means spiritual insight and receptivity, not optical eyesight (“it'd be ridiculous for Jesus to say the world can't accept him because the Holy Spirit isn't 3D”).",
    "Spiritual “seeing” (Raymond Brown & NLT)",
    ["Greek verb of sight = spiritual insight: In John 14:17 (“because it neither sees him nor knows him. But you know him, for he lives with you and will be in you”), the verb for “see” refers to spiritual discernment, not physical vision. The world is unreceptive and lacks the proper spiritual attitude — not lacking physical eyesight to spot an invisible ghost.",
     "Raymond Brown's Commentary: Renowned Johannine scholar Raymond Brown explicitly notes that the world's inability to receive him is an issue of attitude, not literally being unable to see the Holy Spirit — the Greek verb of sight covers either bodily or spiritual insight.",
     "NLT translation confirms this sense: “The world cannot receive him, because it isn't looking for him and doesn't recognize him. But you know him, because he lives with you now and later will be in you.” The disciples were spiritually prepared because Jesus foretold him to them; the world was neither looking for him nor ready to recognize him."],
    ["John 14:17 (NLT)","Raymond Brown, Commentary on John"],
    [],
    {quotes:[{by:"New Living Translation (John 14:17)",en:"The world cannot receive him, because it isn’t looking for him and doesn’t recognize him. But you know him, because he lives with you now and later will be in you.",ref:"John 14:17 (NLT)"}]})
  ],
  {facts:[["Departure Condition","John 16:7 (Spirit already present in Luke 1:15)"],["Same Title","Jesus is paraklētos (1 John 2:1)"],["Same Verb (menō)","John 14:17 & 15:7 (words abiding)"],["Scholar","Raymond Brown: “see” = spiritual attitude"]],
   quotes:[
    {by:"Luke 16:29",en:"They have Moses and the Prophets; let them hear them.",ref:"Luke 16:29"},
    {by:"John 16:13",en:"He will not speak on his own; he will speak whatever he hears, and he will declare to you the things that are to come.",ref:"John 16:13"}
   ]}),
 sum:"John 14–16's Paraclete comes only after Jesus leaves, speaks only what he hears, and “abides” and is “seen” in the exact spiritual/teaching sense of Luke 16:29, John 15:7, and Raymond Brown's commentary — fitting Muḥammad ﷺ, not an already-present Spirit."});

